<title>JORDAN: Jordan M1 money supply falls 2.8 pct in September.</title>
<headline>Jordan M1 money supply falls 2.8 pct in September.</headline>
<dateline>AMMAN 1996-11-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Jordan's broadly defined M1 money supply fell 2.8 percent to 1,593 million dinars in September from 1,640 million in August, the Central Bank statistical bulletin said.</p>
<p>				  (All figures in millions of dinars)</p>
<p>				  Sep 1996  Aug 1996     Sep 1995</p>
<p> Currency in circulation     979     1,015	  1,127</p>
<p> Money Supply (M1)	   1,593     1,640	  1,815</p>
<p> Money Supply (M2)	   5,168     5,253	  5,142</p>
<p>------------------------ ANNUAL INDICATORS ----------------</p>
<p>				  (All figures in millions of dinars)</p>
<p>				  1995    1994     1993    1992</p>
<p> Currency in circulation   1,051   1,073    1,048   1,004</p>
<p> Money Supply (M1)	   1,746   1,746    1,730   1,716</p>
<p> Money Supply (M2)	   5,160   4,814    4,482   4,193</p>
<p>NOTE -- The Central Bank defines M1 and M2 as:</p>
<p>M1 - currency in circulation and dinar sight deposits.</p>
<p>M2 - currency in circulation, including demand deposits in dinars and deposits of municipalities, public entities and non-banking financial institutions.</p>
<p>Economists attribute tighter liquidity in the banking system to a tough monetary policy pursued by the Central Bank to ensure a stable dinar, boosting foreign reserves and quelling inflationary pressures.</p>
<p>$1=0.7080 dinar</p>
<p>- Amman newsroom 962 623776</p>
